%% Two registries, two trust models — Ways to Build & Deliver Apps.
%% The trusted-content contrast the demo probes live from a terminal: registry.access.redhat.com
%% serves UBI to a Pod on this cluster with no credentials at all, while registry.redhat.io answers
%% the same anonymous request with 401 until the cluster's global pull secret is presented. Both
%% feed the SAME build, and what both buy is provenance — the image the build tags into the
%% project's ImageStream is made of layers whose origin can be named and whose patches have an
%% owner. Wording follows concept.adoc's "Where your image comes from: provenance and trusted
%% content"; the two behaviours are the ones the step's `oc run probe` one-liner demonstrates.
%% Shape (diagram-size-guard): LR, four ranks, no subgraph. In LR a rank is as wide as its widest
%% LABEL LINE, and `registry.access.redhat.com` is the longest line here, so the two registries
%% share rank 1 and the credential difference rides on the edge labels rather than adding a rank.
flowchart LR
  UBI["registry.access.redhat.com<br/>UBI base images"] -->|"no credentials<br/>HTTP 200"| BUILD
  RIO["registry.redhat.io<br/>the fuller catalog"] -->|"cluster pull secret<br/>401 without it"| BUILD
  BUILD["S2I build<br/>Java 21 builder"] --> IMG["ImageStream tag<br/>in your project"]
  IMG --> PROV(["Provenance: every layer<br/>named, maintained, patched"])
  classDef reg fill:#e00,stroke:#900,color:#fff;
  classDef built fill:#e6f4ea,stroke:#137333,color:#0d652d;
  class UBI,RIO reg;
  class BUILD,IMG,PROV built;
